---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/apps/smsbump';
const title = 'SMSBump for Shopify: history, retirement and record continuity';
const desc = 'Trace SMSBump into retired Yotpo messaging, reconcile archived subscriber and workflow records, and separate Reviews and Loyalty from the old SMS service.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/apps">← All app reviews</a>
    <p class="badge mt-6">Supplier history · checked November 25, 2026</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">SMSBump: identify the retired service behind your records</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">An old SMSBump invoice, popup or automation name can still appear in a Shopify store after its sending service has gone. Use this guide to trace that history and establish which system now owns each subscriber, sender and workflow. SMSBump is no longer a new SMS purchase.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>SMSBump, Yotpo SMS and the end of messaging</h2>
      <p>Yotpo announced the <a class="link" href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/smsbump-joins-yotpo/">SMSBump acquisition in January 2020</a>. Its later <a class="link" href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/yotpo-smsbump-bringing-the-power-of-yotpos-platform-to-sms/">Yotpo SMS &amp; Email announcement</a> connects the SMSBump history to that branding. These names describe supplier continuity; they do not identify three current services to install alongside one another.</p>
      <p>The <a class="link" href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">2025 migration announcement</a> scheduled Email and SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025. The current <a class="link" href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/">decommissioning notice</a> confirms that the SMS dashboard and background services are inactive. Historical MMS, keyword, cart recovery and suite descriptions are not present-day feature or price offers.</p>
      <p>Reviews and Loyalty are separate continuing products. Keeping either does not restore SMSBump sending. For the wider module migration decision see the <a class="link" href="/apps/yotpo-email-sms">Yotpo Email &amp; SMS guide</a>; for a new sending destination use the <a class="link" href="/alternatives/yotpo-email-sms-alternative">replacement selection guide</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Build a provenance ledger from what survives</h2>
      <p>Start with archived exports, old invoices, Shopify app records, popup screenshots, sender registration correspondence and any replacement provider's import log. Record source name, account or store identifier, capture date, destination system and missing fields. Ask support what records remain recoverable; an inactive dashboard is not an export plan.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Subscriber evidence:</strong> retain the original number, capture source, wording and timestamp alongside withdrawals and import dates. A migration date is not a new opt-in date. Keep email and SMS status separate.</li>
        <li><strong>Segments:</strong> distinguish an exported list of Gold members from the live rule that originally selected them. Record which product supplied tier, purchase and review fields, and which source now updates them.</li>
        <li><strong>Sender and replies:</strong> locate the number or sender identifier, registration owner and any transfer confirmation. Have the destination confirm ownership, reply routing and opt-out processing before assuming the old identity moved.</li>
        <li><strong>Messages and assets:</strong> preserve approved copy, product links, image rights and offer expiry. Do not reuse an old cart link or coupon merely because the template imported successfully.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Mark a missing permission record as unresolved rather than reconstructing it from an order or loyalty enrollment. Keep the affected record outside the sendable audience until eligibility is established. Reconcile phone formats and duplicates without discarding the most recent withdrawal.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Translate four old workflows into current ownership</h2>
      <p>These are proposed continuity checks, not observed SMSBump tests or merchant results. Keep a row for the old job, its replacement, event source, dispatch checks and cancellation owner.</p>
      <p><strong>Beauty cart with an image:</strong> retain the serum SKU and original offer intent. In the replacement, check current stock, product URL, permitted image use, destination MMS support and billed segments. Compare plain text and image variants only after the message is eligible; historical click claims cannot justify an MMS premium.</p>
      <p><strong>Post-delivery review request:</strong> identify whether Reviews or the new sender schedules the request. Use delivery evidence or a documented fallback, verify the order and product, and cancel a duplicate or completed request. A review record can supply context without supplying SMS marketing permission.</p>
      <p><strong>Gold-tier early access:</strong> resolve the live Loyalty tier at dispatch, then separately check SMS eligibility and suppression. A static SMSBump VIP export can be stale after returns or tier changes. Keep the reward rule with the Loyalty owner.</p>
      <p><strong>Home-goods cart after email:</strong> name the current email and SMS owners, whether Sequenzy or another sender is involved. Purchase or withdrawal must cancel pending reminders across both. An unopened email and a weekly suppression export do not establish current eligibility. Replay a duplicate cart event and verify that it creates no second job.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Close the old service record without discarding useful history</h2>
      <p>Check for abandoned signup widgets, old keyword instructions, webhook subscriptions and tracking links in live templates. Replace customer-facing instructions only when their destination works. Record removed integrations and their replacements; retain billing and cancellation evidence rather than assuming retirement settles every account obligation.</p>
      <p>A jewelry store with thin margins should reconstruct contribution from its own orders, discounts, goods cost and messaging invoices. No merchant here was observed needing a 22% discount or moving to Sequenzy. For illustration, a $100 order less $45 goods cost, $20 discount, $8 fulfillment and $2 messaging leaves $25 before fixed overhead. Attribution still does not prove the text caused that purchase.</p>
      <p>Finish with a signed-off ownership list: current sender, preference source, reply owner, Reviews request owner, Loyalty data owner and unresolved records. Archive the ledger so the next operator can explain why a record was imported, suppressed or held back. Evaluate current suppliers on the replacement workload rather than an old SMSBump ranking or guessed monthly bill.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
